The problem asks us to find the number of connected simple undirected graphs with $N$ vertices (labeled 1 to $N$) and $M$ edges such that the number of vertices with even shortest distance from vertex 1 is equal to the number of vertices with odd shortest distance from vertex 1. We need to do this for all $M$ from $N-1$ to $\frac{N(N-1)}{2}$, modulo a prime $P$.
The constraints are $N \le 30$ and $P \approx 10^9$.

Let $d(v)$ be the shortest distance from vertex 1 to vertex $v$ in the graph.
The condition is $|\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}| = |\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}|$.
Since there are $N$ vertices in total, this implies that exactly $N/2$ vertices have even distance and $N/2$ vertices have odd distance.
Note that $d(1) = 0$, which is even. So vertex 1 is always counted in the even set.
Thus, we need exactly $N/2 - 1$ other vertices to have even distance, and $N/2$ vertices to have odd distance.

